DC-Cam staff visit the Museum of Economy and Currency of Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): On September 6, 2022, 32 staff members of the Documentation Center of Cambodia visited the Museum of Economy and Currency of Cambodia to learn about the history of the use of Cambodian currency. DC-Cam staff are divided into two groups and two guides from the Museum of Economy and Currency of Cambodia present.
The museum is divided into 12 different locations, highlighting key facts and lessons drawn from Cambodia's economy and monetary history, and highlighting the close relationship between currency, credit, economic growth, and inflation.
However, the use of Cambodia's currency was completely abolished when the Khmer Rouge came to power on April 17, 1975. Not only that, the Khmer Rouge regime almost completely destroyed Cambodia's infrastructure and human resources. After the fall of the Khmer Rouge regime on January 7, 1979, the Cambodian currency was reprinted and put on the market.
Through this visit, the staff of the Documentation Center of Cambodia will be more aware of the importance of using the Cambodian currency while Cambodia is promoting the widespread use of the Cambodian currency or riel.
Stong District Governor Calls on Local People to Vaccinate Their Livestock
(Kampong Thom): Mr. Say Kim Lam, Governor of Stong District, in cooperation with the Production and Veterinary Office of the Department of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries of Kampong Thom Province, conducted a campaign to vaccinate livestock in Stoung District this morning. This 2022.
On this occasion, Say Kim Lam urged the local people to get their animals vaccinated against various infectious diseases as notified by the village animal health agency and relevant authorities.
He confirmed that the launch of the vaccination campaign today was to vaccinate more than 200 animals in Ponhea Chey village, Banteay Stong commune, Stong district. In addition, for other villages and communes, the village animal health agency will continue to vaccinate the people until the end.
He urged farmers to be extra vigilant with their livestock as some areas are flooded and if there is any disease on cattle to report to professional officials for timely examination and treatment.
He said that vaccination of cattle must be done through officials to the local people or through the village veterinary agency, and all vaccinations do not require people to pay. No.
Mr. Say Kimlam advised all pet owners to clean their pets regularly and monitor the health of their animals. If their animal health is abnormal, please contact or report immediately to the animal health agency. The village or local authority, village chief, commune chief in time, they can help treat and prevent the spread of infection in the community.
