Through this campaign, 52 restaurants and eateries in the 6 target provinces have promised not to serve wild meat to their guests.
The Zero Trap Campaign in Ratanakkiri Province was launched on August 4, 2022 yesterday under the chairmanship of Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment and Chairman of the Zero Trap Campaign, with the participation of Mr. Nhem Sam Oeun, Governor of Ratanakkiri Province, Provincial Council, Judiciary, Partner Organization, Director of Provincial Department of Environment, Armed Forces, as well as more than 500 people and students in Banlung City.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra said that after the completion of the first phase of the campaign in the six provinces east of the Mekong River, the Ministry of Environment will continue to lead partner organizations to campaign for zero traps in some provinces in the southern Cardamom Mountains. There are also Kampong Speu, Koh Kong, Pursat, Battambang, Kampong Chhnang and Sihanoukville.
At the same time, Mr. Net Pheaktra confirmed that in the future, the Ministry will evaluate the data obtained and will continue to implement the second phase of this campaign.
In addition, Mr. Neth Pheaktra said that for the Zero Trap campaign launched in the six target provinces does not mean to end the campaign completely, but this campaign will be like rain to mainstream. Understand more and more people at all levels about the values and importance of natural resources and wildlife in Cambodia.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra stated: "For the Northeastern region, the six provinces, we will continue more activities with new plans with new goals, and with new activities to serve the old goals, our old goal is to provide "Safe for wildlife and reduce traps in protected areas."
Separately, Mr. Seng Teak, President of WWF Cambodia presented the report prepared in 5 provinces, which has been prepared 5 times in the past, with the direct participation of 2,595 participants in the past 5 provinces. People. There are representatives from the government, provincial authorities, civil society organizations, communities and people, students in each province.
The Conservative said that one of the highlights of the campaign was made in each province by promoting through the tuk-tuk about the importance of the campaign by the provincial authorities and partner organizations, which spread for 30 days. In addition, the radio media in the province also participated in broadcasting in each province.
Mr. Seng Teak highlighted: "Some social media and YouTube have spread the message about this zero trap campaign. "So in general, the message that was disseminated to the people through the data we recorded contained more than 300 news articles and headlines published by national and international media."
At the same time, he said that according to the evaluation in more than 300 news articles, this article has spread to more than 3 million people in our district, which we have recorded. Through this promotion, there has been a lot of positive feedback from the public, including feedback on social media.
According to the president of the organization, the past five campaign activities have been organized similarly in each province. Through this campaign, the team has launched some important technical reports such as: deer report, vulture report and elephant conservation report. The team also put up billboards advertising the campaign in all target provinces and organized campaign campaigns to promote the campaign in each province.
He expressed hope that through this campaign, there will be more education on the impact of the trap crisis on people and communities. The main result of this campaign is how the Ministry of Authorities and civil society work together.
Ratanakkiri provincial governor Nhem Sam Oeun said that on behalf of the provincial administration, he fully supported the zero-trap campaign.
The provincial governor confirmed that he would link the campaign to public forums and meetings of provincial and municipal administrations at each additional meeting to promote the importance of the zero trap campaign.
Please be informed that the Ministry of Environment has initiated and led relevant units, national and international organizations, working partners on conservation, zero trapping campaign, which has so far been conducted in 6 provinces, including: Stung Treng, Preah Vihear, Kratie, Mondulkiri, Kampong Thom. Ratanakkiri province just launched the Zero Trap campaign on September 4.
The Bangkok Post reported, citing Visit Limlurcha, honorary president of the Thai Food Processors Association, that the increase was driven by the economic recovery and the resumption of business activities after easing restrictions. Tightening Kovid-19 as well as growing concerns about food shortages in many countries as a result of the armed conflict between Russia and Ukraine.
Visit said the economic recovery in many countries has led to an increase in business activity, while an increase in travelers and a recovery in tourism have led to an increase in food demand. And food services.
In particular, food service providers are rushing to increase their food stocks after orders fell during an outbreak.
Visit added that concerns about food shortages in many countries as a result of escalating geopolitical conflicts have also led food importers to accelerate food imports as the depreciation of the Thai baht has pushed up food exports. Thailand has been competitive this year.
According to the Bangkok Post, Thai food exports generated 797 billion baht in the first half of this year. Of total exports, agri-food exports rose 21% year-on-year to 372 billion baht, while agro-industry fell 40% to 398 billion baht.
Thailand's top 10 food import markets are China, ASEAN, the United States, Japan, India, South Korea, the United Kingdom, Australia, Hong Kong and the Netherlands. Food products that saw strong growth in the first half included fresh fruits, frozen fruits and dried fruits, up 20% from the same period last year to 118 billion baht.
