(Mondulkiri): Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Spokesman for the Ministry of Environment Forests in protected areas where all parties need to be involved in tackling this trap to ensure the safety of wildlife, both habitats and water sources.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra made this statement on September 2, 2022, during a visit to the Srek Pok Wildlife Sanctuary in Koh Nhek District, Mondulkiri Province to review the study. Wildlife of the Mondulkiri Provincial Department of Environment and WWF.
Exploring the management and conservation of natural resources, including forests and wildlife in Sre Pok Wildlife Sanctuary, the largest mixed forest area in Southeast Asia, with a total area of more than 370,000 hectares.
According to a study by experts from the Department of Environment and WWF, the number of rare animals in the Srepok and Phnom Prech wildlife sanctuaries is still facing a significant decline. The decline is due to the main threats, traps, the use of live and processed guns to shoot wildlife, and the illegal use of hunting dogs.
He said that although the number of toenails is declining, at the same time, some species of wildlife, including the white-tailed macaque, are endemic and increasing, and birds such as vultures Re-emerging in Sre Pok Wildlife Sanctuary.
The Secretary of State said that all parties are continuing their efforts to conserve wildlife and put in place various measures, including legal and economic measures.
According to the Mondulkiri Provincial Department of Environment, in 2021, more than 12,000 traps were removed from the Phnom Prech Wildlife Sanctuary and the Phnom Prech Wildlife Sanctuary. Trapping factors have been threatening biodiversity in protected areas.
Mr. Seng Teak, President of WWF Cambodia, said that the eastern landscape is an unprocessed diamond with high natural tourism potential, especially in the Sre Pok Wildlife Sanctuary, a mixed forest area that is home to toenails. In particular, leopards, which represent 60% of the world's leopards, live in the wildlife sanctuary in Mondulkiri.
He stressed the need to manage and conserve the area to increase biodiversity.
During the visit, a team from the Ministry of Environment and WWF inspected the field data collection by researchers.
