G7 industrialized nations to restrict Russian oil prices to prevent Moscow from reaping huge profits from rising energy prices
BERLIN (Reuters) - The G7 industrial power said on Friday that it would make "urgent changes" to restrict the price of oil imports from Russia in a bid to prevent Moscow from reaping huge profits from rising energy prices. This is according to the release by Reuters on Friday, September 2, 2022.
Finance ministers from the Group of Seven (G7) countries said in a statement that they would immediately work to complete and implement the measure, without specifying restrictions.
He added that the purpose of pricing oil exports was to "stop a major source of funding for the war of aggression and curb rising global energy prices."
The finance minister said the G7 sought to establish a "comprehensive coalition" of support for oil prices to increase the effectiveness of the measure.
European Chamber of Commerce in Cambodia promotes green economy development to attract investors and compete with regional countries
(Phnom Penh): The European Chamber of Commerce in Cambodia (EuroCham) has promoted green economic development by calling for increased use of solar energy and the full implementation of renewable energy to attract investors and compete with regional countries. .
Tassilo Brinzer, President of the European Chamber of Commerce in Cambodia, said that the Royal Government's full approval and support for the use of solar panels on the roof was a major step towards modernizing Cambodia's manufacturing base. Let Cambodia gain a reputation as a green and more competitive environment and be able to better integrate itself into the regional economy.
"This will push Cambodia to become an investment destination because investing in renewable energy can be supported as a qualified investment project in the new investment law and attract producers with clean energy goals," he said. In fulfilling its goals.
Rogier van Mansvelt, vice-chairman of the European Chamber of Commerce's Green Business Committee in Cambodia, said most garment factories were under pressure from buyers to install solar panels. If Cambodia wants to maintain its attractiveness to garment investors, it should develop regulations and tax rates that support solar installations.
"The Royal Government of Cambodia has engaged in relevant and important discussions on this issue and we hope that the Royal Government will seize the opportunities provided by renewable energy identified in this document," he said. "International buyers and the sector need more renewable energy."
According to documents from the European Chamber of Commerce in Cambodia, the use of solar energy in industrial estates and factories, as well as increased investment in this sector to help Cambodia compete with Vietnam and Thailand.
One of the other major benefits, in addition to reducing carbon emissions, is the potential for creating new industries that guarantee a better future in sustainable energy, led by the development of solar technology. The government can also save on operating costs and capital costs associated with energy costs by promoting a wider market for solar panels.
