Ms. Kong Chantho, the girl's mother, described that her 18-year-old daughter was an outstanding student with a B-level exam and a scholarship at Pannasastra University for free for up to four years. Even more so. She arranged to rent a room for her children to study in Phnom Penh, but her daughter disappeared.
Ms. Kong Chantho stated that she and her family were shocked when they lost their daughter and tried to find their beloved daughter. Suddenly one day she saw a picture of her daughter in Khem Veasna's video sitting in the field listening to Khem Veasna speak.
After seeing the picture in the video, Ms. Kong Chantho held a photo to find her child in Mr. Khem Veasna's farm, but has not seen her child yet. She has been waiting for her baby on the way to Khem Veasna's farm for more than 10 days.
A mother embraces a photo of her daughter in the heat of the day, waiting for the baby to be born and raised. The dropout was caused by a teacher named Ou Ol, who had studied with him and was a member of Khem Veasna's Coalition for Democracy.
According to Chantho, another friend of her son was also dragged into Khem Veasna's farm.
Ms. Chantho expressed her nostalgia for her daughter and slept waiting for her daughter. "I miss my daughter so much," she said. So far, not less than 100 kilometers of land to ride every day.
She also expressed regret that her son was dragged away from school. "I feel sad that my son is a student who has to go to school and he drags me to the middle of the jungle," she said. I am deeply saddened to lose the future of my child who worked hard for 12 years, got good grades and turned to inspiration. "I feel sorry for him."
Not only Kong Chantho's daughter was dragged to live on Khem Veasna's farm, there were many young Cambodians who were dragged away from their families, dropped out of school and quit their jobs. These actions have seriously affected the future of Cambodian youth, affecting the interests of society, family and society as a whole.
Ms. Chantho called on the Royal Government to seek justice and help prevent sowing and dragging. Ms. Chantho appealed, "Let the Royal Government help find justice like me, the mother and other mothers who broke up their families due to inspiration. There are teachers, there are factions, they are the drag. Only 10 fingers, let the Royal Government help find justice for my family, as well as many relatives who have complained about the loss of children, lost husbands.
Ms. Than Tho also called on the government to intervene for her daughter to return home and resume her studies.
