Ministry of Labor spokesman: More than 500 Cambodians in Japan and South Korea quit their jobs and study to return home because they believed in Khem Veasna
(Phnom Penh): At least 500 Cambodians, including more than 400 in South Korea and more than 100 in Japan, have quit their jobs and returned home after believing in such propaganda. Religion of Khem Veasna, President of the Coalition for Democracy.
Heng Sour, spokesman for the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training, confirmed to Fresh News that between August 23 and 25, there were more than 400 Cambodians in South Korea and in Japan. About 100 Cambodians, including one doctoral student in Kansai, Japan, stopped returning.
He added that some returned on the grounds that their parents were seriously ill in order for their employers to allow the law. Others asked the boss, but the boss refused, stopped illegally, gave up his job and returned to Cambodia following the unfortunate supernatural propaganda of Mr. Khem Veasna.
However, he added that abroad from August 27, 2022, there is no movement to give up work back to Cambodia.
He added that to resolve the issue, the ministry met with representatives of the Korea Human Resources Development Unit (HTD Korea) to apologize to the Korean government, especially Korean employers affected by the unrest. The Cambodian embassies in Korea and Japan, as well as stakeholders, also visited the affected Korean and Japanese workers and employers.
"At the same time, we urge Korean and Japanese employers to have the utmost understanding for our workers and trainees not to sue our brothers and sisters and to consider accepting our workers and trainees as much as possible." This is according to Mr. Heng Sour.
