Own Oxygen Generator Makes Siem Reap Referral Hospital Efficient and Costs Reduced
(Siem Reap): Oxygen is an essential need for hospitals to save lives. Currently, Siem Reap Provincial Referral Hospital has installed 3 oxygen generators donated by Mr. Hun Manet and 1 Japanese person to supply the needs in the hospital and reduce the cost.
Mr. Kros Sarath, Director of the Siem Reap Provincial Health Department, said on August 29 that Mr. Hun Manet had donated three large oxygen generators to the Siem Reap Provincial Referral Hospital. One machine in half an hour can produce 16 bottles of oxygen and 32 bottles per hour can produce oxygen, a single machine to the hospital has up to 3 machines, so the hospital can use enough.
And this oxygen machine can be produced for use in hospitals and distributed oxygen to district hospitals and health centers that lack oxygen can be brought here free of charge. The hospital also has another oxygen machine donated by the Japanese government, but this small machine can only produce 2 bottles per hour.
Separately, Mr. Pen Phalkun, Director of Siem Reap Provincial Referral Hospital, said that in the past 15 years, the hospital has developed many specialized services that are necessary for effective treatment. After the development of specialized services, both human resources and equipment are modernized to ensure the provision of quality, safety and efficiency.
After the smooth use of this service, many patients came for treatment until the number exceeded the number of hospitals, and hospitals in other provinces also sent patients in severe cases for treatment. Siem Reap has the same capacity as the National Hospital, with hard work in emergency surgery, especially blood transfusion.
He added that next year in August, there will be a new hospital building with a capacity of 220 beds, six operating rooms, emergency rooms and more treatment rooms. Currently, Siem Reap Provincial Referral Hospital is at Level 3 (CBA3) with all services, but the hospital also needs to be further developed due to the high demand for medical treatment.
