Garlic farmers selling in Tbong Khmum province's Tambor district can earn a better income for the family economy
Kouk Srok Green Agricultural Community Farmers in Kork Srok village, Kork Srok commune, Tambor district, Tbong Khmum province, about 60 families prefer to grow garlic because it is the most important source of household income. Also. Every day, community farmers harvest 2 to 3 tons of garlic and sell it to the market every day, gradually improving the lives of farmers.
Mr. Choeun Long said that growing garlic has some difficulties if there is a lot of rain, water, onion rot, fungal diseases and pests. However, the green farming community of Kork Srok has provincial agriculture officials and community leaders providing cultivation techniques, leveling the soil, maintaining irrigation water, applying natural chemical fertilizers, compost, and pest control to the farmers so that they can be successful. This crop.
At the same time, Mr. Ten Tum, 32 years old, President of Kok Srok Agricultural Community, stated that in his community, there are about 60 families who grow vegetables such as garlic, leaf, cucumber, watermelon, tomato, eggplant, soybeans and so on. On an area of 64 hectares.
His community has been around for more than two years, but the process of growing vegetables has grown significantly, especially garlic leaves sold to the market in Suong, Tbong Khmum, Kampong Cham and Phnom Penh, not less than 2 to 3 tons per day. This rainy season. In the dry season, 3 to 4 tons per day can be sold for 3,000 to 3,200 riels.
Growing garlic can provide jobs for villagers who can earn between 20,000 and 30,000 riel per person in just one morning. According to the Green District Agricultural Community, the successful cultivation of vegetables by farmers has improved the lives of farmers.
