Demolition Map in South Taksin Village Demolition Activities Tree Planting Activities at Demolished Areas Mekong River Commission: Don Sahong and Xayaburi Hydropower Project Affects Mekong River Water and Fisheries
The Mekong River Commission released its research report last week on the effects of the Don Sahong and Xayaburi dams in Laos.
Through pilot testing, the program has studied two new dam projects, the Don Sahong and Xayaburi MRC projects, which aim to develop monitoring methods and standards that all dam operators on the Mekong River can use. Applicable in the future.
Finally, the monitoring team also observed some changes in the diversity of fish in the upper part of the Xayaburi Dam, with a steady level from 2017 to 2019 and a decline in 2020. At the bottom of the reservoir, this diversity is still high. In contrast, yields appear to be stable, with increases reported in 2017, declining in 2018 and 2019, and increasing again in 2020.
Around the Don Sahong Dam, observers have spotted a surprising amount of data: in recent years, catch yields in northern Cambodia have declined, but have risen sharply in the lower reaches of the dam. Back.
"We need to be aware of the level of our challenges and determine the most effective way to assess this impact," said Dr. Anulak Kithikhoun, Executive Director of the MRC Secretariat. While this report shows some of the impacts of the dam, it is too early to assess whether all the impacts are caused by the dam. "Climate change and other developments could also be factors."
The Mekong River Commission said the joint environmental monitoring report was part of a larger MRC effort representing member countries, including Cambodia, Laos, Thailand and Vietnam, to strike a balance between economic and social benefits. The impact of the dam is not only on the environment, but also on the millions of fishing families and farmers who depend on the Mekong River for their livelihood.
For future projects, the report also calls for "systematic monitoring of water during all stages of construction and operation to take appropriate action in the event of poor water quality."
