Prayuth was the military commander-in-chief when he led a coup to overthrow Yingluck Shinawatra in 2014. He has since led the country as head of the military regime and then became prime minister of the civilian government in 2019 after the general election.
Opposition Pheu Thai recently filed a petition demanding that the Constitutional Court decide exactly how long Prayuth can stay in power. Opposition groups say Prayuth's term as prime minister began in August 2014, months after the military coup, and that it should end before the end of this month after eight years. According to the Thai constitution, the prime minister can serve a maximum of eight years.
But some supporters have argued that his term as prime minister had only begun in 2017, when the new constitution came into force or after the 2019 general election, meaning that Prayuth should be allowed to stay in power indefinitely. By 2025 or 2027, if he still gets enough votes in parliament.
The Thai Constitutional Court was first established by the Constitution in 1997. Under the rule of law and democracy, the tribunal has the following main powers and responsibilities:
1. To consider and adjudicate on the constitutionality of laws or draft laws; 2. To consider and adjudicate on issues related to the duties and powers of the National Assembly, the Senate, the Council of Ministers or independent organizations. 3. The other duties and powers of the Constitutional Court under the Thai Constitution are to review the constitutionality of the draft constitutional amendments and to receive complaints related to the Constitution.
* Good and bad scenarios for Prayuth
In the worst case scenario, the Constitutional Court will rule that Prayuth's eight-year term as prime minister has expired, starting in 2014, according to the opposition. But do not forget that Prayuth has another influential position after the post of Prime Minister, the Minister of Defense, which means that he will remain influential in Thai politics.
In this scenario, both houses of the Thai National Assembly will begin the process of electing a new prime minister from among the candidates proposed by the major parties in the 2019 general election. The new prime minister and cabinet will remain in office until the end of March 2023, when general elections are expected. Acting Head of State issues Royal Decree appointing two Secretaries of State of the Ministry of Posts
