The largest opposition party in Thailand and two-thirds of Thais polled believe Prayuth Chan-o-cha, who first came to power through a 2014 military coup, should step down. And August 24 is Wednesday this week because his term as head of the military government also includes the duration of his term as prime minister. According to the Thai constitution, the prime minister can serve a maximum of eight years.
Thai police on Tuesday cordoned off areas around the Prime Minister's Office in central Bangkok, deploying roadblocks, including the use of containers, and facilitating traffic. Mr. Prayuth also arrived at the office to hold a weekly cabinet meeting.
Currently 68 years old, Prayuth Chan-ocha was the military commander-in-chief when he led a coup to overthrow Yingluck Shinawatra in 2014. He then became the Prime Minister of the civilian government in 2019 after the general election. The next general election is expected to take place in May 2023.
But now the Pheu Thai opposition has filed a petition demanding that the Constitutional Court decide for sure how long Prayuth will remain in power, as the court may choose to accept or reject the Pheu Thai petition on Wednesday. This. Opposition groups say Prayuth's term as prime minister began in August 2014, months after the military coup, and that it should end before the end of this month after eight years.
