Samdech Techo Hun Sen introduced this during the graduation ceremony for more than 3,000 students of Asia-Europe University on Tuesday morning, August 23, 2022. During the ceremony, Samdech Techo Hun Sen spoke about the economic crisis in Sri Lanka, leading to protests to overthrow the country's leaders.
Regarding the crisis in Sri Lanka, Samdech Techo Hun Sen revealed that a part of the crisis is due to the high cost of paying salaries to civil servants and the armed forces. Samdech Techo Hun Sen immediately instructed to research the number of civil servants and the armed forces in this country.
Through contacts in Sri Lanka, Senior Minister Osman Hassan received data that Sri Lanka, with a population of nearly 22 million, has nearly 2 million civil servants and armed forces.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen considers the number of civil servants and the armed forces in Sri Lanka to be a large number, which may be one of the reasons leading to the economic crisis in Sri Lanka.
"Normally, only 4% of GDP is spent on individual officials, and if the Sri Lankan crisis is studied, the personal burden may be too much, not 4 to 10 percent," he said. To 30 percent of GDP. ”
Regarding the issue of Sri Lanka, Samdech Techo Hun Sen said that opening Cambodia as an expert will lead to the removal of individuals to alleviate the burden.
Based on his experience in Sri Lanka, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the head of the Royal Government of Cambodia, advised not to recruit too many civil servants and members of the armed forces. According to Samdech Techo, Cambodia currently has a total of more than 400,000 civil servants and the armed forces.
In addition to reminding the recruitment of civil servants and the armed forces to work, Samdech Techo Hun Sen also advised to be strict in the selection of civil servants, that is, to select competent people and clear exams.
The 2022 National Budget Law limits the minimum wage and the salaries of civil servants and the armed forces in 2022 to the same level as in 2021 without any increase.
Maintaining the salaries of civil servants and the armed forces so that the Royal Government can still save money to continue health and social interventions. In particular, increase the budget to help the poor and vulnerable to 9 consecutive months in 2022.
At the same time, the Royal Government also guarantees the timely payment of salaries to civil servants, twice a month, without deducting the salaries of all types of civil servants in 2022.
The minimum wage levels of all civil servants and the armed forces in 2022 will be maintained as follows:
