China provides $ 29 billion in special loans to troubled real estate developers
The size of the lending program, announced with little detail by the Ministry of Housing, the Ministry of Finance and the People's Bank of China on Friday evening, will be the largest financial commitment from Beijing to Coping with the perceived real estate crisis that has caused house prices to fall and property sales to plummet.
Hundreds of thousands of middle-class Chinese families are stranded after paying off mortgages and borrowing money from banks to buy real estate, which the cash-strapped developer is now struggling to complete. Some homebuyers have begun boycotting amortization as a threat to social stability during a time of political sensitivity to a change of leadership of the Chinese Communist Party later this year.
A source familiar with the matter told Bloomberg on condition of anonymity that the People's Bank of China and the Chinese Ministry of Finance would inject money through policy banks such as the China Development Bank and the Agricultural Development Bank of China.
This special loan will be used only for houses that have already been sold but have not yet been completed.
