Minister Prum Sokha Encourages Blood Donation to Save Lives
(Phnom Penh): On the morning of August 22, 2022, Mr. Prum Sokha, Minister of Public Works and Chairman of the Royal School of Administration, participated in the Blood Donation Program to save lives at the Royal School of Administration.
According to the welcoming speech by Mr. Youk Bunna, Permanent Secretary of the Ministry of Public Works and Director of the Royal School of Administration, the Royal School of Administration has organized this blood donation program twice since 2016, with a total of generous volunteers. 129 people, 10 women and 3 monks, consisting of monks, leaders, professors, government officials and other philanthropists.
He said that the blood donation program runs throughout the day from morning to afternoon, with the list divided into two shifts.
Mr. Youk Bunna stated that the Blood Donation Program to Save Lives is a model social activity that will help promote and enhance the quality of training within the framework of the Royal School of Administration in line with its motto for discipline, virtue, quality. And innovation.
Speaking on the occasion, Mr. Prum Sokha said that the lack of blood to save the lives of patients is a challenge that is given the most attention. The Royal Government, through the Ministry of Health, has strived to develop a policy framework for blood donation and a law governing blood donation to encourage and encourage all types of people to donate 100% of their blood voluntarily before 2020, which is Joint Recommendations of the World Health Organization.
It should be noted that in 2021, we need a block of blood to save the lives of patients every 6 minutes. The total daily blood requirement is more than 250 blocks on average in Phnom Penh. Nationwide demand is more than 100,000 units, an increase of nearly 20% over previous years, and the National Blood Transfusion Center of the Ministry of Health covers more than 70% of the country's blood supply.
Free and voluntary blood transfusions meet only about 10% of the demand in our country, while neighboring countries can supply more than 90% of the voluntary blood donation. Acting Head of State: Cambodian-Vietnamese leaders must continue to maintain the tradition of solidarity and friendship to be a legacy for future generations, according to the World Health Organization.
