(Phnom Penh): Cambodian and Thai oil and energy officials have expressed a desire to continue negotiations on overlapping oil fields to benefit both countries and peoples.
Thailand and Cambodia will continue to negotiate over the OCA to jointly benefit from new oil resources in the region, which will help mitigate the impact of price fluctuations, Thai energy officials say. Oil and energy after 20 years of delays in negotiations
He said Thailand needed to secure more oil supplies for electricity generation after declining oil volumes in the Gulf of Thailand led to an increase in imports of expensive liquefied natural gas (LNG).
Although he did not specify Cambodia's position in the negotiations, he wanted the outcome to be fair to both sides.
"It is very complex, both politically and economically. We have tried to talk with the Thai side, split it in two, leaving the border issue in part, and let's develop natural resources together, modeled by the countries," he said. Other countries have similar maritime border disputes. "Thailand has a problem with Malaysia and he has done it successfully."
Negotiations to resolve the dispute over the overlapping claims have been postponed for 20 years after the two countries failed to negotiate in 2001.
The overlapping claim area covers more than 26,000 square kilometers, which is estimated to contain millions of barrels of oil and gas on the seabed.
Mr. Chiep Sour stated that in the past, companies have also studied the potential of natural resources in this area, but not yet in depth, so if negotiations on joint development in this area is necessary to study. More details.
Since the early 1970s, Cambodia and Thailand have each claimed their rights to develop overlapping areas.
