Vietnamese traders rush to buy Khmer rice at Kampong Touk, Kampong Ror district military canal, Prey Vol international gate
(Svay Rieng): Cambodian farmers are rushing to transport rice to sell at Kampong Dor, a canal, a soldier's wing, at the Prey Vale international border crossing, as Vietnamese traders bring large Pok Chai boats across the border to buy rice while Vietnamese traders are short of rice. Exported to third countries.
According to the authorities stationed at the military canal ferry port, for almost two months from July to August 20, 2022, Vietnamese traders have been short of rice to produce rice for export. Large lice cross the border into Cambodia through the waterways of the military canal at Prey Vall village, Thmey commune, Kampong Ror district to buy rice from our Cambodian traders and farmers.
According to the authorities, there is little rice because the rainy season is not ripe yet, while the dry season rice expires in early July, and Vietnamese traders have a high demand for rice to produce rice for export to third countries, so Vietnamese traders bring boats across the border. Park waiting to buy our Khmer rice.
The same source said that recently, Cambodian traders and farmers have been transporting rice to sell in Kampong Kanot, Canal Troops, by truck, by truck, waiting for Vietnamese traders to bring in boats to buy rice and transport rice to Vietnam. .
People say that when the rainy season is harvested from October to November, Vietnamese traders come to collect and Vietnam starts to lower the price of Cambodian rice, because the Cambodian market is completely dependent on them (Vietnam).
People living along the border say that if Vietnamese traders do not come to buy rice, there will be more risks, so in order to keep the rice market at market value on the Cambodian farming community, people have requested the Department of Agriculture, Ministry of Agriculture, Ministry of Commerce Relevant authorities increase rice procurement from farmers and increase rice production for export to ensure a better rice market for the people who produce it.
