Launch campaign to clear illegal structures in Angkor and other areas under the control of APSARA Authority
(Siem Reap): A large-scale campaign to measure the identification of plots, identification of land occupants and illegal constructions in the Angkor resort area and other areas under the control of APSARA Authority has started on 18 August 2022. .
Mr. Chea Sophara, Deputy Prime Minister, Minister of Land Management, Urban Planning and Construction and Chairman of the Orientation Team to Address Illegal Construction and Land in Angkor Resort and Other Areas Under the Control of APSARA Authority, led the working group. Live.
Deputy Prime Minister Chea Sophara visited a number of important villages near Angkor Wat, Area 1 and Area 2, such as Veal Mondul 3 and South Taksin.
The campaign to measure and determine the norms of plots and identities of illegal occupants of land and structures in the Angkor resort, which is managed by the APSARA Authority, lasts for 15 days from August 18 onwards. The campaign has a total force of 779 divided into 32 groups, including 439 officials from the Ministry of Land Management, 232 officials from the Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts and 108 officials from the Siem Reap Provincial Administration.
Deputy Prime Minister Chea Sophara said that there are currently more than 8,000 cases of illegal construction in Angkor National Park. In the last three months, a total of 398 cases of illegal construction have occurred, of which more than 200 cases have been dismantled.
The ministry has set strict conditions for the owners of all these illegal constructions to be removed immediately. Meanwhile, the Siem Reap Provincial Administration and the APSARA Authority continue to take measures to prevent the construction and demolition of illegal structures, prohibit the purchase and sale of land and houses of all kinds, and do not provide any legal documents in the Angkor resort area.
Deputy Prime Minister Chea Sophara also warned of legal action against those protesting against the illegal construction.
The Angkor site, which was inscribed on the World Heritage List in 1992, covers an area of 401 square kilometers.
The campaign aims to preserve the Angkor site, an ancestral heritage, national identity and soul, as well as the universal value of World Heritage properties for all mankind.
