This morning, Samdech Techo Hun Sen received 300,000 doses of 19 Spanish vaccines against Kovid.
(Phnom Penh): On the morning of August 18, 2022, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, received the Kovid 19 vaccine with the help of the Spanish government and people. The ceremony will take place at the Peace Palace.
The 19-dose Pfizer Kovid vaccine, given to 300,000 children by the Spanish government and people, arrived in Cambodia just after midnight on August 17, 2022. Ms. Youk Sambath, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Health, went out to receive and prepare the vaccine throughout the night. According to Ms. Youk Sambath, the vaccine is a bilateral aid.
Ms. Youk Sambath said that so far Cambodia has received a total of 74 million doses of vaccine and more than 7.6 million doses are left in storage.
Japan's trade deficit widens as imports hit record high
Japan's imports hit a new record high in July, driven by global oil inflation and a weaker yen that weighed on exports and narrowed trade deficits. Growing karma is a sign of further trade decline for export-dependent countries. According to Reuters.
The trade data came after the release of the Reuters Tankan Business Index, which showed an improvement in business sentiment in Japan in August, as a major corporate capital expenditure index rebounded. June from the fall in May.
Although mixed data provides some evidence of a recovery, policymakers are likely to continue to call for more stimulus measures as the world's third-largest economy struggles with repercussions. By epidemics and as global vision declines.
"Japan's export-led economy will lose momentum this year and early next amid fears of a global downturn," he said.
Exports rose 19 percent in July from a year earlier, the 17th consecutive month of growth, led by auto exports to the United States, Japanese Finance Ministry data showed on Wednesday. US and chip-related products exports to China This is higher than the forecast of 18.2 percent.
Imports rose 47.2 percent in July from a year earlier to a record 10.2 trillion yen ($ 76.06 billion), driven by higher crude oil prices. Rocks and liquefied natural gas. The increase was higher than the forecast of 45.7 percent and far higher than exports, leading to a trade deficit of 1.44368 trillion yen in July.
The data also showed a 23.1 percent drop in the yen against the same period last year pushed up import costs. According to Reuters.
