Wild Honey Expects to Expand Market After Receiving Quality Certification
(Phnom Penh): Private sector and wild honey harvesting community officials expect the wild honey market to expand after obtaining certifications that meet quality and hygiene standards that enable the product to compete with imported honey. Currently, imported honey is circulating in the Cambodian market.
He added that the certification will further expand the wild honey market by providing customer confidence and boosting market competition, especially with imported honey products, as he observed that honey imports from abroad began to increase. Many of them are a challenge for the community to compete because the honey is cheaper.
"By providing this certification, it will help expand the market for them and make the public more aware of wild honey, because some people do not know much about wild honey, think that honey is not pure, but it is Different techniques. When it comes to wild honey, it helps us a lot, helps our health.
"There are a lot of honey products in Cambodia that are in circulation, but today we learned that wild bee products have a certification mark that focuses on the safety of their honey harvest," he said. "He is part of pushing his products into a wider market than any other product."
He urges communities and companies to implement safety systems such as GNP, Good Manufacturing Practices and HACCP, a food safety certification system to gain greater confidence.
Ms. Sreng Chhun Leang, from the community of Krala Peas village, Stung Treng province, who received the quality certification, stated that the reason why her community received the certification was because the community community followed the quality standards, complied with the conditions and hygienic packaging. .
She expects the wild honey market from her community to be bigger. The honey market in the Kralapas community in 2022 is better than last year, with more than 300 liters sold this August, up from last year, as the community began to advertise and sell on Facebook.
According to the Natural Forest Company, there are currently 25 community farmers harvesting forest water and more than 600 members. Most of the wild honey that can be harvested is in Kratie, Stung Treng, Preah Vihear and Koh Kong Provinces.
