Deputy Prime Minister Chea Sophara warns of arrests of protesters over illegal construction in Angkor
(Siem Reap): Deputy Prime Minister Chea Sophara, Minister of Land Management, Urban Planning and Construction, has specifically warned residents to build illegal structures in the Angkor resort area to dismantle them on their own, rather than using the authorities' force.
According to the Minister of Lands and the chairman of the working group to address the problem of illegal construction in the Angkor resort area, there are currently more than 8,000 cases of illegal construction in Angkor National Park. In the last three months, a total of 398 cases of illegal construction have occurred, of which more than 200 cases have been dismantled.
Through the working group to measure and identify the land now, Deputy Prime Minister Chea Sophara expects to be able to effectively prevent illegal construction. The Royal Government of Cambodia has prepared land in the area of Run Ta Ek Natural Village for the people who will have to dismantle those illegal structures and give them legal land ownership.
