Trapping has become a crisis and poses a serious risk to wildlife living in Southeast Asia, as well as the risk of animal-to-human transmission of the virus.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment and Chairman of the Trap Zero Campaign, said that through this campaign, the owners of 47 restaurants have announced to stop selling and cooking wildlife meat to special customers, providing knowledge and understanding. To the public to stop eating all kinds of wild meat.
In addition, Mr. Neth Pheaktra stated that Cambodia has set up mechanisms as well as laws and practices on a regular basis to prevent and combat such illegal wildlife trafficking, crack down on many crimes and liberate many wildlife from the clutches. The perpetrators also arrested and sent the case to court to be punished according to the law, as well as fines.
"Natural resources can grow without people, but people can not live without natural resources, so it is necessary to pay attention to this," said Neth Pheaktra.
"Because it is love, and I want Cambodian fans to love nature, wildlife, change their attitude to do good things, help society and spread to the public, especially from her fans on social media and pictures. Others to understand the invaluable value of natural resources and wildlife. This is the commitment of Meas Soksophea.
The popular Cambodian singer also plans to mobilize her fans to participate in large-scale natural resource conservation campaigns in the future to raise awareness among local people.
He said that through joint activities to protect natural resources and wildlife in Cambodia, CI has worked closely with rangers, including supporting rangers who are performing their duties in the four areas to provide opportunities for visits. Patrol, including removing traps from the forest. The organization works with local communities to encourage people to change their attitudes to participate in the conservation of natural resources and to provide employment through ecotourism.
"I would like to call on the people and stakeholders to work together with conservation organizations and the government to reduce the pressure on wildlife resources by removing traps," he said. And answer no to eating wild animals together.
In particular, Mr. Chanthanasak, Officer of the Fauna International Fauna Program in Cambodia (FFI), said that wildlife and humans are facing traps in natural resource protection areas. Traps are not only the killers of small wildlife, but also large elephants should be trapped, according to a study in the Cardamom Mountains, at least three baby elephants were killed. Trapped until paralyzed.
Mr. Chanthanasak further urged to participate in further outreach to the people and stakeholders to stop trapping wildlife, we must all stop eating wildlife meat, while wildlife meat does not know what it eats, so meat Wild animals are full of many vicious viruses in their bodies.
"Together we will not eat wildlife, we will eliminate wildlife traps and eliminate all kinds of poaching," said Chhit Sam Ath, USAID Representative.
Please be informed that the Ministry of Environment, in collaboration with partner organizations, has launched a six-month zero trapping campaign in six provinces, including: Stung Treng, Preah Vihear, Kratie, Mondulkiri, Kampong Thom and Ratanakkiri. So far, zero trapping campaigns have been held in five provinces. The campaign was held in Kampong Thom province on August 12, 2022, the fifth target province and had hundreds of participants.
Commander-in-Chief of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces (RCAF) Receives Lao Military Attach
(Phnom Penh): On the morning of August 15, 2022, General Vong Pisen, Commander-in-Chief of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces (RCAF), paid a courtesy call on Lt. Col. Phim Mak Chanvong Panha, Director of the outgoing Lao Joint Attorney General's Office in Cambodia. On that occasion, Colonel Vilay Sorn Sisumbat, the newly appointed Director of the Lao Military Attaché, also paid a courtesy call.
During the conversation, Colonel Phim Mak Chanvong Panha thanked His Excellency the Commander-in-Chief for allowing him and his team to pay a courtesy call and say goodbye on this occasion.
Colonel Phim Mak Chanvong Panha said that for more than three years, he has been interested in and highly appreciated Cambodia's progress under the leadership of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia.
He also thanked the leadership of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces (RCAF) for their close cooperation in all aspects of the success of his diplomatic mission in Cambodia.
In response, General Vong Pisen praised Colonel Phim Mak Chanvong Panha's military diplomacy mission and welcomed the newly appointed Colonel Vilay Sorn Sisumbat as Director of the Lao Military Attachment Office in Cambodia. .
General Vong Pisen highlighted a number of situations in Cambodia, including the successful fight against Kovid 19 and its ability to control community outbreaks and reopen the country's economy.
General Vong Pisen also recalled the traditional relations that the two countries have had for a long time and now continue to maintain good relations and cooperation on behalf of the two countries.
In the field of military, the Commander-in-Chief highlighted the good cooperation between the two countries' Ministries of Defense and Defense through the signing of a Memorandum of Understanding (MoU), training on human resources and exchange of visits.
The Commander-in-Chief also thanked His Excellency the Chief of Staff of the Lao People's Army for attending the 19th ASEAN Commander-in-Chief Meeting and believed that through the new Chief of Staff The two armies will continue to strengthen solidarity, friendship and good cooperation.
