The body of Minister Khieu Kanharith's mother-in-law was cremated at Wat Langka by Samdech Preah Van Rath Noy Chrek
(Phnom Penh): Samdech Preah Van Rath Noy Chrek, Samdech Preah Sang, Second Deputy Director of the Kingdom of Cambodia and Preah Chao Athikar Wat Chan Borey Vong, offered the cremation of Kosal Chetta, a Buddhist disciple of Pen Yut, who is the mother-in-law of Minister Khieu Kanharith. Information and mother of Ms. Khieu Tep Rainsy Kanharith at Wat Lanka Preah Kosomaram in Sangkat Boeung Keng Kang I, Khan Chamkar Mon, Phnom Penh on the morning of August 14, 2022.
Disciple Kosal Chenda Puthsasanupathak Pen Yut, mother-in-law of Khieu Kanharith, Minister of Information and founding mother of Khieu Tep Rainsy Kanharith, always practiced good deeds in both the realm and the realm of Buddhism.
"As a Cambodian citizen, the great disciple Kosal Chenda, a Buddhist monk, always practices good deeds in both the realm of the realm and the realm of the Buddha," he said.
Rev. Kosal Chenda, a Buddhist monk, was born in 1930 and had a father named Tep Pen and a mother named Oeung Yap, a single daughter. Under French colonial rule, the devotee studied at the elementary level and then as an adult, Vathanaka mastered the skills of tailoring and design until she built a life as a husband and wife with Dr. Tep Tho, a health officer. A senior officer of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces during the Sangkum era.
All the children of Rev. Pen Yut have fulfilled their role as intellectuals, capable citizens to participate in social work, public welfare, both in the Sangkum Reastr Niyum era and in the Techo era after the January 7 victory until today. .
Under the genocidal slavery regime of Pol Pot, Pen Yut and his family were evacuated from Phnom Penh to live as new people under the weight of torture and starvation at the cooperative in Traing Krom village, Ta Ek commune, Khsach Kandal district, Kandal province. Millions of people were evicted from their homes at the time.
After the liberation on January 7, Rev. Pen Yut actively participated with her husband in the rehabilitation and reconstruction work in the field of health and medicine by joining Calmette Hospital. During that time, Rev. Pen Yut also served directly with Lok Chumteav Phlek Phirun, President of the Cambodian Red Cross during the People's Republic of Cambodia and in the following decades.
After advancing into middle age, especially the progress of the Kingdom of Cambodia, Venerable Disciple Pen Yut purified his body and sacrificed his life to follow the path of Buddhism by being a monk at Wat Mongkul Van (called Wat Svay Dangkum) until The end of life on August 9, 2022.
Disciple Pen Yut has made great contributions to both Buddhism and the Empire in supporting the temples of Wat Svay Dangkum, Phnom Penh, Wat Thmor Kor, Lvea Em, Kandal Province, and the social sector in many places in Phnom Penh and Kampong Cham.
