At the same time, Mr. Neth Pheaktra said that Cambodia currently has between 400 and 600 Asian elephants, most of which live in the Cardamom Mountains, the northern plateau of the Tonle Sap (Prey Lang) and the eastern highlands in the province. Mondulkiri, Ratanakkiri Province, is a rich biodiversity area with ecosystems and slopes conducive to the habitat of elephants and other wildlife. The number of domestic elephants is estimated at more than 70.
The spokesman for the Ministry of Environment confirmed that Cambodia is actively involved with countries around the world in conserving the world's remaining elephants and is listed as endangered on the Union Red List. The International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) has called for an end to all activities that threaten elephants, especially to reduce human-elephant conflict.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra said: "Asian elephants are under global threat due to poaching, trapping, human-elephant conflict, and domestic breeding, which we all share. "It is necessary to participate in the protection and conservation of elephants."
