(Phnom Penh): People who commit human trafficking and are arrested by the police and sent to the judiciary rarely escape punishment according to the law, because the authorities always find a lot of evidence to put the burden on those people.
This was stated by Mr. Keng Somarith, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Justice in the press conference this morning, August 12, 2022 of the Spokesperson of the Royal Government under the theme "Situation of human trafficking in Cambodia."
Mr. Keng Somarith said, "We always observe that perpetrators who commit human trafficking are rarely released, rarely escaped the law because we have focused and worked hard. "From the possibility of finding evidence to supplement the case, which is a solid basis for the judge to decide the sentence for that person."
A senior official of the Ministry of Justice claimed that the punishment for the perpetrators was to seek justice for the victims and to decide on compensation for the victims for what they had suffered. In addition, the perpetrator is imprisoned so that the individual does not commit such crimes to others.
The legal expert added that the punishment is severe or light depending on the nature of the perpetrator and the Ministry of Justice does not have the authority to ask the judiciary to decide more or less. Must be decided in accordance with the law.
However, Mr. Keng Somarith added that in the event that there is no law to punish some activities that the public perceives as human trafficking, the Ministry will cooperate with relevant institutions to study and draft a law to control and crack down. Activities that are perceived as those offenses or if there are shortcomings may lead to amendments to supplement them.
Please be informed that in 2021, the Royal Government of Cambodia cracked down on human trafficking more than any other year, despite facing 19 epidemics of Kovid. In that, more than 359 cases were cracked, while in 2019, 155 cases were cracked, 1,577 victims were rescued, while in 2019, there were 467 cases, 538 cases were sent to court.
