Vietnam will need to invest up to 14 billion USD a year in energy until 2030 (Video inside) (Hanoi) Billion dollars a year until 2030 to develop new power plants and expand its grid. According to Reuters.
Reuters quoted Deputy Minister of Industry and Trade Dang Hoang An as saying in a statement that 75 percent of the money would be spent on new power plants, with priority given to sources. Renewable and 25% will be spent on expanding the grid.
The minister said Vietnam would seek funding from private investors for the new energy project, but gave no details.
Vietnam, a regional manufacturing hub, needs to increase its energy production capacity by about 10 percent a year to support its fast-growing economy and population.
Vietnam, which promised last year to become a carbon neutral country by 2050, will increase its offshore wind power generation capacity by 7 gigawatts (GW) by 2030 and to 65 GW by 2045, An said. While reducing the proportion of coal in its energy mixture.
"Vietnam will not add new coal-fired power plants to its main energy development plan and will only continue running coal projects under construction until 2030," An said.
