The Northern Tonle Sap Lowland Protected Area is an area of seasonally flooded grasslands and is located in Kampong Thom and Siem Reap provinces. According to a study by wildlife scientists, this area is important for hummingbirds to live and reproduce.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra's remarks were made on the occasion that he led a joint team of journalists to visit the Whistle Conservation Project in the Northern Tonle Sap Landscape Protection Area in Bralay Commune, Stong District, Kampong Thom Province on the 11th of this month. This August 2022.
On that occasion, the senior official said that in order to support the conservation of the hummingbird, the Ministry of Environment has cooperated with the partner organization WCS to implement the Sustainable Rice Project, work related to the protection and conservation of the hummingbird nests. As well as providing cattle for the community to raise as part of the community's economic development as part of the protection and conservation of whispers.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra continued that with the efforts of the Ministry of Environment and NGOs, new businesses are being provided to the people so that they can avoid encroachment on protected areas as well as wildlife hunting. Sustainable rice projects in connection with the principle of no encroachment, no hunting or trapping of wildlife, especially the whispering species.
"Their community is very aware of the importance of the whistle. They also benefit from the whistle through the ecotourism that comes to visit the area. They earn both for the community and "Knowledge for them as a guide, etc."
In addition, Mr. Neth Pheaktra said that according to the canal community report, there are 22 nests of whistleblowers, including 24 whistle eggs that have been successfully hatched in natural habitats. The number of whispers in the wild is increasing.
According to Mr. Meas Than, President of the Canal Community, by promoting and understanding the benefits of the Whistle, people in his community have come to protect the nest and conserve the Whistle without hunting.
He said that prior to the 19 outbreaks of Kovid, his community had earned more than 10,000 US dollars, of which, by bringing in a whistleblower for one visitor, they would pay $ 15. To communities for local development.
However, the head of the community also expressed concern about the loss of life of the squirrel flying on the power line and the sending of buffaloes to disturb the area where the squirrel lives.
Ms. Sorn Sokan, a farmer in the canal community, expressed her great pleasure in participating in the conservation and conservation of the world's endangered bird species, benefiting from this species through tourism and rice participation. Sustainability project.
She said that without the presence of whispers in her area, there would be a lot of loss through tourism, especially the loss of the implementation of the sustainable rice project, which is more expensive than regular rice.
It should also be noted that the Northern Tonle Sap Lowland Protected Area is part of the Tonle Sap Lake Biosphere Reserve by Royal Decree No. NS / RKOT / 0401/070, dated April 10, 2001. On the other hand, the Tonle Sap Lake Biosphere Reserve is receiving a lot of attention from UNESCO.
