China's exports to sanctioned Russia rose 22.2 percent in July from a year earlier in dollar terms, making it the first export growth. Since March, according to Reuters calculations based on customs data released on Sunday. China's exports to Russia in June fell 17 percent.
Growth in Chinese imports from Russia maintained a high growth rate of 49.3 percent in July, though lower than the 56 percent growth in June and 79.6 percent in May.
Russia is a major source of oil, gas, coal and agricultural commodities for China. During the war with Ukraine, Russia was China's largest oil supplier in May and June, while Chinese consumers bought oil from the country at low prices.
However, oil supplies from Russian ports to China fell to 21.3 million barrels in July, the lowest level since February. According to Reuters.
