In addition, the Association of Physicians and Pharmacists Association said that the association will visit and treat people and distribute gifts in Saang district, Kandal province and Svay Teap district, Svay Rieng province. Myanmar cuts official foreign exchange rate to 13.5% for kyat
Myanmar's central bank earlier this week lowered its exchange rate from 1,850 to 2,100 per dollar. According to Reuters.
Myanmar's economy has collapsed since the military seized power last year, halting a decade-long reforms that have put pressure on the dollar against the dollar and boosted oil prices. Fuel and food prices rise.
Reuters reports that the black market exchange rate for currencies is still 20 to 30 percent higher than the new official rate of the Central Bank of Myanmar, according to figures from the online trading group that trades the currency unofficially.
Myanmar's central bank, which has been replaced by more and more senior military officials, this year announced a series of measures to control currency flows.
Local companies and banks are ordered to suspend and re-analyze for the repayment of all foreign loans, deposits or foreign currencies at local banks within one business day. Lower-level administrations were ordered not to use foreign currency for domestic transactions.
Last weekend, Myanmar's central bank eased the requirement, saying that 65 per cent of foreign currency received locally must be deposited in licensed banks, which previously required 100 per cent.
Myanmar's central bank revised the exchange rate in April from 1,778 to 1,850 to the dollar. According to Reuters.
