Pat Sovann, GRET's agricultural technician, said that the project to produce compost from earthworms came at a time when the prices of fertilizers and other agricultural inputs were rising, which would help reduce farmers' costs. On the purchase of fertilizer in part. That is, the production of compost from this worm will contribute to the environment and farmers' economy, as well as safe vegetables.
He said that the project to produce compost from earthworms, GRET has cooperated with the environmental authority of Dum Dek district and Cintri, a garbage collector to collect garbage from this market and the surrounding area to bring to earthworms and produce. Compost for farmers to use on various crops, both vegetables and rice.
"We have already studied the amount of waste that can be recycled into fertilizer in Dom Dek market, and we have cooperated with the local district authorities to ask for his location where we can build a building to store garbage and put worms, as well as compost storage," he said. . And this location may be built in the next two to three months. GRET will start training vegetable sellers in the steel market so that they understand how to sort and store garbage separately. ”
He said that the fertilizer produced will be used in the vegetable production community in Sot Nikum district, Siem Reap province, if the production volume exceeds the demand, the project will also sell to farmers outside the community.
According to the agricultural technician, he expects to collect about 1 ton of garbage or other waste per day to produce fertilizer, which can produce about 20 tons per year or more than 2 tons per month.
