Experts say support for local vegetables and meat products helps reduce malnutrition for children and women
(Phnom Penh): Experts say that support for locally produced vegetables and meat products will help reduce malnutrition for children and women and help the economy of local farmers, while the Royal Government and development partners are currently Encourage and train farmers to increase vegetable production according to safety standards to meet local needs.
She said that among the many causes of malnutrition in children and adults, eating imported vegetables that contain chemicals also causes malnutrition.
She said that the use of locally grown vegetables will contribute significantly to reducing malnutrition for women and children, because most local vegetables are grown without the use of chemicals and grown to safety standards.
Ms. Thoeun Sopheary, Nutrition Officer of the Siem Reap Provincial Health Department, said that eating safe vegetables is richer in nutrients than vegetables that use chemicals and do not affect the health of women, children and the general public. For pregnant women, eating green leafy vegetables is best because it is rich in iron and other nutrients and micronutrients.
She encourages farmers to increase the cultivation of safe vegetables to meet the demand and encourage consumers to support the cultivation of vegetables grown to safety standards without the use of chemicals. If there is more support, it will encourage farmers to grow to meet more market demand.
Mr. Pat Sovann, GRET Agricultural Technician, said that GRET has been working with farmers in Siem Reap since 2010 to promote sustainable vegetable growing by training farmers on cultivation techniques, composting and irrigation. Safe and organic crops by switching to chemical crops to meet safety standards in response to consumer nutrition.
