A Cambodian woman entrepreneur who makes ripe brown ice cream gets support from customers and encourages people to know how to process local items to promote Cambodian products
Lor Srey Leak is just 32 years old, born into a family that has been making ice cream for more than 20 years. She collected ripe palm fruit from farmers in Preah Dak village, Preah Dak commune, Banteay Srei district, Siem Reap province, for 500 riel to 1,000 riel per fruit, depending on the size of the palm. The ripening palm season is from May, June and July, which is collected, peeled and boiled, and then stored in a freezer bag when needed to make brown ice cream.
At the same time, she also encouraged all citizens to learn how to process local goods or agricultural products to avoid importing too much from abroad, which will result in the loss of jobs and part of the income of the people. Both want to promote the potential of Khmer palm to tourism.
Currently, Khmer ripe brown ice cream is sold at the Khmer product market, where most foreign tourists come to try and taste. As a new product, ripe brown ice cream has received support and praise from customers that it is unique and delicious. In addition to selling at stalls, Lor Srey Leak also produces and distributes in a number of shops and restaurants in Siem Reap.
