EU regulations covering the plan approved by the 27-nation bloc two weeks ago were published in the EU's official administrative journal on Monday and will take effect on Tuesday.
The purpose of the regulation is to allow the EU to increase its gas reserves in time for what could be a difficult winter. European households and businesses are struggling with rising energy prices and declining Russian gas supplies, on which many member states depend.
The regulations say EU countries will do their best to reduce gas consumption by at least 15 percent between August this year and March next year, based on the figures they have consumed on average in Five years ago.
