Opposition groups in Thailand are planning to petition the Constitutional Court to decide whether or not Prayuth's time as leader during the military regime should be included during the current civilian term. . Under the Thai constitution, a person can serve as prime minister for a total of eight years.
A new poll by the University of Thai Nida (NIDA), which was attended by 1,312 people from August 2-4, found that 64 percent of the people wanted Prayuth to step down on August 2. August 23 marks only eight years after he became the leader of the military government through the 2014 coup. Meanwhile, a poll released on Sunday showed that 33 percent of the vote was pending a decision by the Constitutional Court.
In the past, Prayuth has also faced opposition-initiated parliamentary elections, but he has won all four. There have also been months of youth-led protests against his leadership and against the monarchy in Thailand.
It should be noted that Mr. Prayuth Chan Ocha served as the head of the military government from 2014 until 2019, after the general election, then the Thai Parliament decided to elect him to continue as Prime Minister. According to the Thai constitution, the next general election is expected to be held no later than 2023, but so far no official announcement has been made as to when it will take place.
