Mr. Neth Pheaktra Urges Organizations Implementing Rural Education Project in Siem Reap to Increase Humanitarian and Environmental Activities for Local Communities
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Spokesperson of the Ministry of Environment, urged members of the Cambodian Rural Students Trust (CRST), which is educating and providing scholarships to poor school children in rural Siem Reap, to increase their activities and efforts. Intensify humanitarian and environmental work for local communities.
Mr. Net Pheaktra's promotion took place on the morning of Sunday, August 7, 2022, when he presided over the reception of the Rotary Club in Siem Reap, a member of the Cambodian Rural Students Trust.
Taking that opportunity, Mr. Neth Pheaktra emphasized to the members of the Cambodian Rural Students Trust that for Cambodia, peace is invaluable and very important for the nation and its people. Cambodia achieved complete peace on December 29, 1998 under the implementation of a win-win policy laid down by Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia.
"Due to this complete peace factor, Cambodia has ample opportunity to build a prosperous country, develop economic growth, rebuild human resources and rebuild Cambodia's reputation," said a senior official at the Ministry of Environment. Therefore, we must join with the Royal Government of Cambodia to maintain the peace that Cambodia has to survive.
Mr. Pheaktra said that the Cambodian Rural Students Trust is an important part of the Ministry of Environment, which has worked together to make the local community in Siem Reap a plastic-free community. The project introduces clean swimming pools in schools and distributes water bottles to students for daily use as part of improving the environment, especially reducing plastics in their communities.
Rotary is a global network of more than 1.4 million members that provides services to others, promotes integrity and promotes awareness of the world, goodwill and peace through professional businesses and community leaders. The International Club also works to promote peace, fight against infectious diseases, provide clean water, health and hygiene, rescue mothers and babies, support education, boost the local economy, protect the environment and encourage participation.
Please be informed that the Cambodian Rural Students Trust is a non-governmental, non-profit organization founded by Aviv Palti and his Australian family and registered with the Cambodian Ministry of Interior in 2011 with the vision of eliminating inequality. Poverty through education, training and promoting young people to become future leaders.
Currently, the organization participates in social work through various projects, including: scholarships for poor students, housing projects for the poor, projects to promote girls and women, bicycle distribution to students in rural areas, no plastic village project. And water and sanitation projects. Arrest of the perpetrator according to the court order after escaping for more than 5 months for throwing acid on the victim
