Central Bank of Thailand to test digital currency for retail later this year
BANGKOK: The Bank of Thailand (BOT) said on Friday it expects to launch a digital currency trial for retailers from the end of this year to mid-2023 as a new payment option. According to Reuters.
During the trial, the Bank of Thailand (CBDC) digital currency will be used for transactions such as cash, such as payment for purchases, according to a statement issued by the Central Bank of Thailand. Goods and services in a limited area and among about 10,000 retail consumers.
The Bank of Thailand will assess the benefits and risks of the pilot project to develop relevant policies and improve design, the statement said.
The Bank of Thailand does not currently have any plans to officially issue its CBDC, the statement said.
CBDC for retail is a digital currency form issued by the Central Bank of Thailand that is comparable to physical banknotes. It can be used in both online and offline financial transactions.
However, the Bank of Thailand has said it does not support the use of digital assets such as bitcoin as a payment for goods and services due to the risks involved. According to Reuters.
