(Phnom Penh): ASEAN has decided not to allow Myanmar to attend the ASEAN Summit scheduled for the end of 2022 in Cambodia unless there is sufficient progress.
This was stated by Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation of Cambodia and Special Envoy of the ASEAN Chair on Myanmar, at a press conference on August 6, 2022 at the Ministry of Foreign Affairs.
"We urge the Myanmar authorities to take concrete steps to implement the five-point unanimous agreement reached by ASEAN leaders on April 24, 2021 in Jakarta," he said. "We also call on all parties concerned to stop activities that exacerbate the crisis and hinder peace talks and could jeopardize peace, security and stability."
According to Prak Sokhon, the ASEAN foreign ministers also reaffirmed their commitment to the five-point agreement to help Myanmar return to normalcy, realizing that lasting peace and national unity can be achieved. Only in accordance with Myanmar's proprietary environmental solution as well as under the leadership of Myanmar.
ASEAN on July 25, 2022 also issued a statement expressing its deep sorrow and grief over Myanmar's execution of four opposition activists.
The four opposition activists who were sentenced to death by the Myanmar military regime include Pujia Yathar, Kyar Min-yu, Lami-yuong and Ong Thuraz, despite requests for reconsideration. Condemn those people directly by Samdech Techo Hun Sen as the chair of ASEAN, as well as requests from other ASEAN member states.
