Commentary: The historical trend of unification on both sides of the Taiwan Strait is unstoppable.
BEIJING, Aug. 2 (Xinhua) - US House of Representatives Speaker Pelosi on Tuesday ignored China's strong opposition and protests and paid a visit to Taiwan. This has led to strong protests and strong opposition to the United States.
Since the ruling Kok Mengtang Party of China withdrew to Taiwan, although they continue to use the names "Republic of China" and "Government of the Republic of China", they have no right to exercise national sovereignty on behalf of China. A long time ago. In fact, they have always been a local authority on Chinese soil.
The Chinese government, which adheres to a firm position and reasonable initiative on the one-China principle, is gaining more and more understanding and support from countries and international organizations, and the one-China principle is widely accepted by the community. International.
On November 15, 1971, former Chinese Deputy Foreign Minister Qiao Guanhua led a Chinese delegation to the United Nations Conference Hall for the first time. Resolution 2758 of the United Nations General Assembly authorizes the People's Republic of China to regain a legitimate seat at the United Nations.
To promote the real progress of relations between the two sides of the Taiwan Straits and to establish cross-strait relations, on November 21, 1990, the Chinese side established the Taiwan Strait Exchange Fund on December 16. In 1991, mainland China established the Taiwan Strait Relations Association.
From April 27 to 29, 1993, Wang Daohan, President of the Taiwan Straits Bilateral Relations Association, and Gu Zhenfu, President of the Taiwan Straits Exchange Exchange, met in Singapore. The Chinese on both sides of the Taiwan Strait have opted for dialogue instead of confrontation, resolving differences through communication and promoting cooperation through negotiations.
The Taiwan Straits Bilateral Relations Association and the Taiwan Straits Exchange Exchange have been authorized to reach the "1992 Common Consensus" orally on the "Taiwan Straits Bilateral Adherence to the One China Principles". "
The core meaning of this consensus is "both sides of the Taiwan Strait belong to China and work together to find national unity." The process and content of the "1992 Consensus" are explicitly documented, and no individual or force can deny its distortion or distortion.
