(Phnom Penh): On Friday morning, August 5, 2022, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, received an Honorary Doctorate in Development from the University of Phnom Penh.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen, one of the leaders of Cambodia, sacrificed all his physical and mental strength and intellect with a deep will, conscience, patriotism and people to build and develop Cambodian society. Cambodia is a country that has been harassed and destroyed by the fires of war.
As a leader with high responsibility for the destiny of the nation and the people of Cambodia, since 1985, Samdech Techo Hun Sen has paid the utmost attention and change. Under the tireless and unselfish efforts of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Cambodia, which is surrounded by the smoke of war, surrounded by poverty, has become a peaceful, developed and prosperous country. Significant progress.
The awarding of an honorary doctorate in development from the Royal University of Phnom Penh is a recognition of Samdech Techo Hun Sen's outstanding achievements in leading, building and developing Cambodian society for the growth and prosperity of its people. An honorary doctorate in development is highly valued by the Prime Minister's unique guide as a great leader and builder of Cambodian society.
At the same time, the Commission thoroughly examined the achievements of Samdech Techo Hun Sen candidates and unanimously decided on January 7, 2020 that the achievements of Samdech Techo Hun Sen candidates in the field of development are high and excellent. Outstanding value in both economics and society at the highest level for receiving the award from the Royal University of Phnom Penh with an honorary doctorate degree in "Development".
Russia used to be India's sixth-largest coal supplier, behind Indonesia, South Africa, Australia and the United States, and Mozambique and Colombia occasionally became the fifth largest suppliers.
India expects the recent approval of its central bank to allow payments in Indian rupee will help boost bilateral trade with Russia. India's imports from Russia have nearly tripled to more than $ 15 billion since Russia's invasion of Ukraine.
However, strong discounts offered by Russian suppliers to Indian consumers in recent months have spurred the purchase of Thermal Coal, which is mainly used for power generation, especially due to global trading prices. Rising close to record high as Western sanctions hurt coal trade
The same data shows that Indonesia is the largest supplier, while South Africa is slightly ahead of Russia. Two Indian traders say higher coal imports from Russia are driven mainly by cement and steelmakers.
