Samdech Techo Hun Sen Asks US to Review Russia Sanctions
(Phnom Penh): The Prime Minister of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, has asked the United States to reconsider its sanctions on Russia, as they affect not only Russia but also Cambodia and the rest of the world.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen's request was made during a working meeting with US Secretary of State Anthony Blinken on Thursday afternoon, August 4, 2022.
Eang Sophalleth, Samdech Techo Hun Sen's personal aide, said that during the meeting, Samdech Techo Hun Sen and the US Secretary of State raised the issue of the Russia-Ukraine war. The US Secretary of State thanked Cambodia for voting in favor of the UN resolution against Russia.
Regarding this election, Samdech Techo Hun Sen stated that in principle, Cambodia adheres to the principles of the UN Charter and the recognized standards related to respect for the sovereignty, territorial integrity and independence of all UN member states. At the same time, Cambodia has always called for a ceasefire, peaceful negotiations and the utmost restraint.
Regarding the Russia-Ukraine war, the Prime Minister asked in front of the US Secretary of State for a review of sanctions against Russia. Samdech Techo stressed that the sanctions affect not only Russia but also the world economy, including Cambodia.
Russia launched an offensive against Ukraine in February 2022. In response to the military operation in Ukraine, European countries, the United States and the United Nations have jointly decided to impose sanctions on Russia.
But the sanctions have also had a devastating effect on economies around the world. Samdech Techo Hun Sen has raised in public forums to review the sanctions.
In a meeting with Joseph Borrell Fontaine, the High Representative for Foreign Affairs and Political Security and Vice President of the European Commission, Samdech Techo Hun Sen also called for a review of Russian sanctions.
Cambodia is developing a policy to attract special tourists to attract participation in socio-economic development
(Phnom Penh): In order to attract and encourage tourists to invest in socio-economic development in Cambodia and to turn ordinary tourists into quality tourists as investors or businessmen, the Royal Government has been preparing a "policy on special tourism" which A new policy to support the growth of tourism in Cambodia.
In order for this new policy to be fully implemented, the Ministry of Tourism has organized a workshop on the draft "Special Tourism Policy" with about 60 ministries, public and private sectors at the Sunway Hotel on the 4th of this month. August 2022 with about 100 participants.
At the opening ceremony, Mr. Tith Chantha, Permanent Secretary of the Ministry of Tourism, stated that the tourists, in particular, are mostly tourists and investors / businessmen who already have businesses and businesses in Cambodia. When they visit their business, they also visit Cambodian tourist destinations and stay longer, spend more money and come more often.
The Permanent Secretary of State reminded that in 2019, Cambodia received about 6.6 million international tourists, about 11 million domestic tourists, a total of nearly 20 million, received about 6 million US dollars in national economy. Of the 6.6 million international tourists in 2019, only 10 percent were tourists / business travelers, so the government wants to increase the amount of quality tourism to 20-30 percent of international tourists.
As the talks began, Permanent Secretary of State Tith Chantha also explained the main objectives of this special tourism policy, with the first aim being to increase the number of international tourists visiting Cambodia. This particular tourism policy will also be a mechanism to attract future tourism growth.
"However, all the ideas are still in the discussion stage before a formal decision in the near future," stressed Mr. Tith Chantha.
The Permanent Secretary of State also suggested that "if tourists have the right to buy land for vacation homes, it should be allowed only in the main tourist areas or in sacred tourist destinations such as Siem Reap, the northeastern coast, which is not "Border areas."
He added that once the special tourism policy is adopted, associations in the tourism sector will have an official document to disseminate to tourists who want to have a second home, family home, as well as foreign investors. Want to have a business in Cambodia or you already have a business in Cambodia who want to have your own vacation home.
